[ECO "E56"]

{In the Gligorić System, the knight often comes to c6 instead of ...b6. On move 7 this is the Bernstein Defense: take on d5 and c5, and Black is the one with the isolated pawn. On moves 8 and 9, the same development and White's isolated pawn give a small edge.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 O-O 5. Bd3 d5 6. Nf3 c5 7. O-O Nc6 {This is the Bernstein Defense.}
(7... dxc4 8. Bxc4 Nc6
(8... cxd4 9. exd4 Nc6 {[%cal Gc6d4] Hits the isolated pawn.} 10. a3 Be7 11. Bf4 a6 12. Rc1 b5 13. d5 {[%cal Gd4d5] The freeing break.} exd5 14. Bxd5 Bb7 {White's pieces are active. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.})
9. dxc5 Bxc5 10. Qc2 {[%cal Gc2h7] The queen and the bishop will aim at h7.} a6 11. a3 b5 12. Bd3 Bb7 {White's pieces are a little more active. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.})
8. cxd5 exd5 {[%csl Yd5] Now Black's d-pawn will be isolated.}
9. dxc5 Bxc5
10. h3 {Takes g4 away from Black's bishop and knight.} Re8
11. b3 Ne4 12. Bb2 Bf5 {White will blockade d4 with a knight and play against the pawn. Stockfish: +0.5, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Leningrad: White's fifth moves"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "5.a3"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Nimzo-Indian Defense: Leningrad Variation"]
[ECO "E30"]

{Against 4.Bg5 c5, White has several fifth moves besides 5.d5 and 5.e3. In each of them the bishop on g5 is loose, and ...h6 and ...Qa5 make use of it.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Bg5 c5
5. a3
(5. Nf3 h6 6. Bh4 g5 7. Bg3 g4 {[%cal Gg4f3] Kicks the knight.} 8. Ne5 Ne4 {[%cal Ge4c3,Ge4g3] Hits c3 and g3.} 9. Qc2 Nxg3 10. hxg3 {Black has the two bishops and pressure on d4. Stockfish: −1.7, clearly better for Black.})
(5. dxc5 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 Qa5 {[%cal Ga5c3,Ga5g5] Hits c3 and g5.} 7. Bxf6 gxf6 8. Qd4 e5 9. Qe3 Na6 {[%cal Ga6c5] The knight takes the c5 pawn next, and White's c-pawns stay weak. Stockfish: −0.3, slightly better for Black.})
(5. Bxf6 Qxf6 6. e3 b6 7. Nf3 Bb7 8. Be2 cxd4 9. exd4 Bxc3+ 10. bxc3 {White's c-pawns are doubled, and Black's bishop rules the long diagonal. Stockfish: −0.4, slightly better for Black.})
5... Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 h6 {[%cal Gh6g5] Asks the bishop to decide.}
7. Bh4 Qa5 {[%cal Ga5c3,Ga5e1] Pins the c3 pawn to the king, as in the Sämisch.}
8. Qc2? {The most common move, but the c3 pawn is still pinned.} cxd4 {The pinned pawn can't take back.}
9. Bxf6 gxf6
10. Nf3 {Black is a pawn up and keeps pressing on c3. Stockfish: −2.3, winning for Black.} *

