{Against 4.Bd2 c5, White has several fifth moves besides 5.e3. None of them is dangerous: Black trades on c3 or d4, castles and plays ...d5.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Bd2 c5
5. a3
(5. dxc5 Bxc5 6. e3 O-O 7. Nf3 d5 8. cxd5 exd5 {[%csl Yd5] This time Black has the isolated pawn, with active pieces behind it.} 9. Be2 Nc6 10. O-O {Stockfish: −0.1, equal.})
(5. d5 exd5 6. cxd5 O-O 7. a3 Bxc3 8. Bxc3 Ne4 {[%cal Ge4c3] Hits the bishop.} 9. e3 d6 10. Bd3 {Stockfish: 0.0, equal.})
(5. Nf3 cxd4 6. Nxd4 O-O 7. e3 Nc6 8. Bd3 d5 9. cxd5 Nxd4 10. exd4 {Black takes back on d5 next. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.})
5... Bxc3 6. Bxc3 Ne4 {[%cal Ge4c3] Hits the bishop.}
7. Qc2 Nxc3 8. Qxc3 cxd4
9. Qxd4 O-O {Black has castled, and White has gained nothing from the opening. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.} *

[Chapter "Three Knights: White's fifth moves"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "5.e3"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Nimzo-Indian Defense: Three Knights Variation"]
[ECO "E21"]

{After 4.Nf3 O-O, four White players in ten play 5.Bg5, the main line. The other fifth moves mostly lead to positions from other chapters: 5.e3 to the Gligorić System, 5.Qc2 to the Classical. Black hits the center with ...c5 or ...d5.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Nf3 O-O
5. e3
(5. Qc2 d5 6. Bg5 c5 7. e3 cxd4 8. exd4 h6 9. Bh4 Nc6 10. cxd5 exd5 {The same position as in Classical: White's fifth moves, after 10...exd5. Stockfish: −0.9, slightly better for Black.})
(5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 c5 7. Bg5 Qa5 {[%cal Ga5c3] Pins the c3 pawn.} 8. Qc2 Ne4 9. Bd2 Nxd2 10. Qxd2 {Stockfish: −0.3, slightly better for Black.})
(5. g3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 b6 7. Bg2 Bb7 {[%cal Gb7g2] The bishops face each other on the long diagonal.} 8. O-O Re8 9. Bg5 d6 {Stockfish: 0.0, equal.})
5... c5 6. Bd3 d5
7. O-O {The same position as in Rubinstein, Gligorić System: 9...b6 after 7.O-O.} dxc4
8. Bxc4 cxd4 9. exd4 b6 {This is the Gligorić System's main line: ...Bb7 and ...Bxc3 come next. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Sämisch: White's sixth moves"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "6.e3"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Nimzo-Indian Defense: Sämisch Variation, O'Kelly Variation"]
[ECO "E26"]

{After 4.a3 Bxc3+ 5.bxc3 c5, White has several sixth moves besides 6.Nf3. The plan stays the same: ...Nc6, ...b6, and ...Na5 or ...Ba6 against c4, with ...d6 and ...e5 when White pushes e4.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. a3 Bxc3+ 5. bxc3 c5
6. e3
(6. Bg5 Nc6 7. Nf3 h6 8. Bh4 cxd4 9. Nxd4 Qa5 {[%cal Ga5c3] Hits c3.} 10. Rc1 Nxd4 11. Qxd4 {Stockfish: −0.1, equal.})
(6. d5 d6 7. Bg5 h6 8. Bh4 exd5 9. cxd5 Nbd7 {[%cal Gd7e5,Gd7b6] The knight heads for e5 or b6.} 10. Nf3 Qa5 {[%cal Ga5c3] Hits c3.} 11. Rc1 {Stockfish: −1.0, clearly better for Black.})
(6. Qc2 Nc6 7. Nf3 d6 8. e4 e5 {[%csl Yc5,Yd6,Ye5] The center closes, and White's bishops have no open lines.} 9. d5 Na5 {[%cal Ga5c4] The knight hits c4.} 10. Bg5 Bd7 {Stockfish: −0.5, slightly better for Black.})
(6. f3 Nc6 7. e4 d6 8. Be3 b6 9. Bd3 Na5 {[%cal Ga5c4] The knight hits c4.} 10. Ne2 Ba6 {Stockfish: −0.2, equal.})
6... b6 {This is the O'Kelly Variation.}
7. Nf3 O-O 8. Bd3 Bb7
9. O-O Ne4 {[%cal Ge4c3] Hits the weak pawn on c3.}
10. Qc2 f5 {[%cal Gf5e4] Supports the knight.}
11. Nd2 {Stockfish: −0.5, slightly better for Black.} *

[Chapter "Kmoch Variation: 4.f3"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "4.f3"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Nimzo-Indian Defense: Kmoch Variation"]
[ECO "E20"]

{4.f3 prepares e4, to build a big center. Black stops it at once with 4...d5. Then castle and hit the center with ...c5. Watch for ...Qh4+: f3 has opened the diagonal to White's king.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. f3 {This is the Kmoch Variation.} d5 {[%cal Gd5e4] Stops e4.}
5. a3 {The most common move.}
(5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. Bd2? {Natural, but the bishop blocks the queen's guard of d4. 6.Qd2 was needed.} Qh4+ {[%cal Gh4e1,Gh4d4] Check, and d4 is attacked.} 7. g3 Qxd4 8. Nxd5 exd5 {Black has won a pawn. Stockfish: −2.0, winning for Black.})
5... Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 O-O
7. cxd5 exd5 8. e3 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits the center.}
9. Bd3 {Next come ...Nc6 and ...Re8. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "Spielmann Variation: 4.Qb3"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "4.Qb3"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Nimzo-Indian Defense: Spielmann Variation, Romanovsky Gambit"]
[ECO "E23"]

{4.Qb3 guards c3 with the queen, like 4.Qc2, but the queen is exposed on b3. Black hits d4 with 4...c5, which also guards the bishop on b4. After 5.dxc5 Nc6 6.Nf3 Ne4, Black wins the pawn back with active pieces.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Qb3 {This is the Spielmann Variation.} c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits d4 and guards the bishop.}
5. dxc5 Nc6 {[%cal Gc6e5] Develops first. This is the Romanovsky Gambit: the pawn on c5 can wait.}
6. Nf3 Ne4 {[%cal Ge4c3,Ge4c5] Hits c3 and c5.}
7. Bd2 Nxd2 {Black takes the bishop pair.}
8. Nxd2 Bxc5 {Black has won the pawn back and has the two bishops.}
9. e3 {Stockfish: −0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Trap, Leningrad: 5.e3? Qa5!"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "5...Qa5"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Nimzo-Indian Defense: Leningrad Variation"]
[ECO "E30"]

{Against 4.Bg5 c5, White's most common move is 5.e3, played in three games in ten on Lichess. The pawn on e3 cuts the bishop on g5 off from d2, where it could break the pin on c3. 5...Qa5! pins the knight, and Black scores three games in four from here.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Bg5 c5
5. e3? {Natural, but now the bishop on g5 can't come back to d2 to break the pin.} Qa5! {[%cal Ga5c3,Gf6e4] Pins the knight on c3. Next, ...Ne4 hits c3 and g5 together.}
6. Bxf6 {The most common reply.}
(6. Qc2 {Guards c3, but not g5.} Ne4! {[%cal Ge4g5,Ge4c3] Hits g5 and c3.} 7. Qxe4 Bxc3+ 8. Kd1 Bxb2 {[%cal Gb2a1] The bishop takes a pawn and hits the rook.} 9. Rb1 Bc3 {Black is a pawn up, and White's king is stuck on d1. Stockfish: −1.4, clearly better for Black.})
(6. Ne2 {The best defense: the knight guards c3 a second time.} Ne4 {[%cal Ge4g5] Now g5 is attacked.} 7. Qd3 d5! {Not 7...Nxg5?: 8.h4 hits the knight, which has no safe square, and White wins it back (Stockfish +0.2).} 8. Bh4 cxd4 9. exd4 {White's pieces are tangled and the king is still in the center. Stockfish: −0.7, slightly better for Black.})
6... Bxc3+ {Takes with check first.}
7. bxc3 Qxc3+ {[%cal Gc3a1] Check, and the rook on a1 is attacked.}
8. Ke2 gxf6 {Black has won a pawn, and White's king is stuck in the center. Stockfish: −1.1, clearly better for Black.} *

[Chapter "Trap, Leningrad: 8.Bh4? g5!"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "8.Bh4"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Nimzo-Indian Defense: Leningrad Variation"]
[ECO "E30"]

{In Leningrad Variation: 4...c5, the knight on c3 was the e4 pawn's main guard, and ...Bxc3+ removes it. After 7...h6, one White player in six keeps the bishop with 8.Bh4?. Then ...g5 breaks the pin, and the knight on f6 takes the e4 pawn.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Bg5 c5 5. d5 d6 6. e4 Bxc3+ 7. bxc3 h6
8. Bh4? {Keeps the bishop, but the e4 pawn is short of guards.} g5! {[%cal Gg5h4] Drives the bishop away, so the f6 knight is free.}
9. Bg3 Nxe4 {[%cal Ge4g3,Ge4c3] Takes the pawn and hits g3 and c3.}
10. Bd3 Nxg3 11. hxg3 Qe7 {Black is a pawn up, and White's pawns on c3 and c4 are weak. Stockfish: −1.1, clearly better for Black.} *

[Chapter "Trap, Classical 4...b6: 7...O-O? 8.e5!"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "7...O-O"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Nimzo-Indian Defense: Classical Variation"]
[ECO "E32"]

{In Classical: 4...b6, about one Black player in seven castles after 7.bxc3. That walks into 8.e5: the knight on f6 must move, and then White's bishop and queen hit h7.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Qc2 b6 5. e4 Bb7 6. Bd3 Bxc3+ 7. bxc3 O-O?
8. e5! {[%cal Ge5f6,Gd3h7] Hits the knight. If it moves, Bxh7+ follows.} Bxg2 {Two Black players in three try this: the bishop hits the rook on h1.}
(8... Ne8 9. Bxh7+ Kh8 10. Be4 {White has won the h7 pawn, and Black's king is bare. Stockfish: +3.4, winning for White.})
(8... Ng4 9. Bxh7+ Kh8 10. Be4 {Again the h7 pawn falls, and the knight on g4 is out of play. Stockfish: +3.3, winning for White.})
9. exf6 Bxh1 {Black has won the rook, but now comes a check.}
10. Bxh7+! {[%cal Gc2h7] The queen now guards h7, so the king can't take the bishop.} Kh8
11. Qd3! {[%cal Gd3h3] The queen heads for h3, where the bishop on h7 can give a discovered check. 11...gxf6? 12.Qh3 leads to mate.} Qxf6
12. Qh3 Re8 13. Be4+ {The discovered check.} Kg8
14. Qh7+ Kf8 15. Bxh1 {White has won back the bishop on h1, and Black's king is under attack. Stockfish: +4.4, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Trap, Sämisch: 9.Qc2? cxd4!"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "9.Qc2"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Nimzo-Indian Defense: Sämisch Variation"]
[ECO "E24"]

{In Sämisch Variation: 5...c5, after 8...Qa5 the c3 pawn is pinned to White's king. Four White players in ten guard it with 9.Qc2?, and one in seven with 9.Qd2?. Both lose material. White had to play 9.Bxf6.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. a3 Bxc3+ 5. bxc3 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Bg5 h6 8. Bh4 Qa5
9. Qc2? {Guards c3, but the pawn is still pinned.}
(9. Qd2? {Guards c3 too, but the queen can be hit.} Ne4! {[%cal Ge4d2] Attacks the queen.} 10. Qe3 cxd4 11. Nxd4 Nxd4 12. Qxd4 e5 {[%cal Ge5d4] Black wins time on the queen, and the c3 pawn is weak. Stockfish: −1.7, clearly better for Black.})
9... cxd4! {[%cal Gd4c3] Wins the d4 pawn. White can't take back with the c3 pawn, because it is pinned to the king.}
10. Bxf6 {The most common reply.} gxf6 {Not 10...d3?, which lets White back into the game (Stockfish +0.4).}
11. Rc1 dxc3 {Black has won two pawns. Stockfish: −2.2, winning for Black.} *

